---
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Gameplay Preview: Farming, Investigation & Replay';
const description =
  'A source-tracked Grave Seasons gameplay preview covering farming grids, the one-year investigation, romance risk, Ashenridge, and replayable killer scenarios.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Gameplay Preview', url: '/gameplay-preview/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steam, SOURCES.steamDevlog, SOURCES.blumhouse, SOURCES.blumhouseMedia];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.farmHarvest.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Gameplay preview - official sources</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">Grave Seasons Gameplay Preview: Farming, Investigation &amp; Replay</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      Searching for <strong>Grave Seasons gameplay</strong> before release usually means asking how
      the farming loop connects to the murder investigation. Official Steam, Blumhouse, and Perfect
      Garbage material gives us the structure, but not a final quest route, crop price table, or
      universal killer solution.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img src={ASSETS.farmHarvest.src} alt={ASSETS.farmHarvest.alt} width={ASSETS.farmHarvest.width} height={ASSETS.farmHarvest.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="eager" />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.farmHarvest.credit}</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-page mt-8">
      <h2>What the official gameplay loop confirms</h2>
      <p>
        Steam describes Grave Seasons as a farming simulation in Ashenridge where a supernatural
        serial killer is active. The player has <strong>one year</strong> to protect potential victims
        and uncover the murders. The store page lists more than <strong>30 residents</strong>, more than
        <strong>70 interiors</strong>, and <strong>seven major locations</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Farming is part of the investigation plan</h2>
      <p>
        The official farming devlog describes four seasons—Spring, Summer, Autumn, and Winter—and
        more than <strong>45 seeds</strong>. It also names Arid, Regular, and Wet plot types, with Wet
        plots not requiring watering in the published system explanation. The visible and invisible
        grids include Items, Crops, NPCs, Objects, Farm Plots, and Doors.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is enough to plan a calendar and a clean evidence sheet. It is not enough to claim the
        most profitable crop, exact growth days, a crop-to-plot compatibility chart, or a best early
        farm layout before the game is playable.
      </p>

      <h2>How the one-year investigation changes the guide</h2>
      <p>
        Because the official premise gives the player a one-year window, a launch walkthrough should
        connect date, resident, location, clue, and consequence. Track the in-game day and time for
        every observation. Keep an official resident or location name separate from a guessed schedule
        or quest trigger.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Log the source for each resident name or location caption.</li>
        <li>Record whether a clue was found before or after a farming or social action.</li>
        <li>Note the exact day, time, weather, and outcome in the playable build.</li>
        <li>Keep a separate list for optional events and possible victim protection.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Replayability means there is no single killer route</h2>
      <p>
        Steam says each playthrough selects a supernatural killer from a candidate pool, with
        killer-specific campaigns, reasoning, kill styles, and targets. It also lists 12 potential
        romanceable people, with a few potentially being the killer. That makes a decision log more
        useful than a one-run “correct answers” page before launch.
      </p>
      <p>
        Romance is therefore not safe to treat as a spoiler-free checklist of guaranteed partners.
        Mark a relationship as confirmed only when the game or a first-party source shows it, and
        record which run or killer scenario produced the result.
      </p>

      <h2>What remains unverified</h2>
      <p>
        Official sources do not yet publish the final quest order, crop prices, growth timers, daily
        schedules for every resident, complete killer roster, victim outcomes, or the optimal farm
        layout. Those are valuable launch-day search topics, but filling them now would turn a preview
        into fiction.
      </p>
    </div>
    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
